-- title:
--   Fibres of a finite integrally closed K[X]-algebra are monogenic
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $B$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain, integrally closed in its field of fractions, equipped with the structure of an algebra over the polynomial ring $K[X]$ such that $B$ is finite as a $K[X]$-module and the $K[X]$-action on $B$ is faithful (equivalently, the structure map $K[X] \to B$ is injective). Let $P$ be an ideal of $K[X]$ which is maximal. Then there exists an element $y \in B$ with the property that every $z \in B$ lies in the coset of the $K[X]$-submodule $P \cdot B$ (the submodule $P \bullet \top$, i.e. the image of $P \otimes B$, written here as the scalar multiple of the top submodule of $B$) determined by some polynomial expression in $y$: explicitly, for each $z \in B$ there is a polynomial $q$ with coefficients in $K[X]$ such that $z - q(y) \in P \cdot B$, where $q(y)$ denotes the evaluation of $q$ at $y$ under the $K[X]$-algebra structure of $B$. Equivalently, the fibre $B/PB$ is generated by the single element $y$ as an algebra over $K[X]/P \cong K$.
--
--   Geometrically, $B$ is the coordinate ring of a smooth affine curve finite over the affine line $\mathbb{A}^1_K$, and the assertion is that each scheme-theoretic fibre of this finite morphism is monogenic (curvilinear) over the residue field at the chosen closed point $P$. It is used in the construction of lifts of normal forms of structure constants for small extensions, where a single generator of the fibre is needed to present $B$ modulo $P$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_exists_aeval_sub_mem_smul_of_isMaximal_of_isIntegrallyClosed.lean

